# Summary of changes for run e6173191-8e23-4acd-a0d3-e982d532e06e
I wrote all five deliverables for the round-42 quadratic-sieve results. Nothing new was proved in this step, and I couldn't compile the LaTeX version because no TeX engine was available.

**Files**
- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,750 words): a popular-science piece. It walks through the congruence-of-squares idea and the "shrink the factor base" proposal. It then explains, in order:
  - why paid sieve work falls as \(Q\) grows, with a worked case where adding 5 to \(\{2,3\}\) at \(X=100\) cuts the work from \(3/20\) to \(4/34\);
  - why Dickman's \(\rho(u)\) can't see \(Q\) (correlation \(+0.029\) versus \(+0.882\));
  - why the factor base is exactly the split set, via the Legendre symbol;
  - the two correct readings of the rank inequality;
  - the \(\mathbb{F}_2\)-rank trap: only test matrices with a nonzero even determinant can catch it, and the error only goes one way;
  - the large-prime channel and its birthday-problem cost question.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,500 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (a shorter amsart version of the same paper). Every definition and theorem is stated in full with a proof sketch:
  - split-set forcing, and the identity \(Q(N,B) = \pi(B) - \#\text{inert}\);
  - the bounds \(2^{|S|}\le\Psi_S(X)\le(\lfloor\log_2X\rfloor+1)^{|S|}\), the step \(\Psi_{S\cup\{p\}}(X)\ge\Psi_S(X)+\Psi_S(X/p)\), and a sieve-yield ceiling with exponent \(Q(N,B)\);
  - the sign-flip criterion;
  - the prefix-rank identity \(k_{\rm success}=\operatorname{rank}(\text{prefix})+1\le Q+1\), and that this bound is sharp;
  - the even-determinant test criterion and \(\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb{F}_2}\le\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb{Q}}\);
  - the large-prime threshold \(a+(b-D)>Q\).

  The paper also covers the methodology and data-integrity findings, five algorithms and future work. The experimental figures (\(2.4\times\)–\(13.1\times\), 2,215/2,215, 180/180, the correlations) are labelled as measurements, not theorems. Neither prose document mentions Lean, formal verification, file paths or proof identifiers.
- **`demo.py`**: a standard-library Python script that reproduces every worked number in the documents, in six sections that follow the paper. It runs in about 2 seconds. One example: at \(B=400\), cutting the columns from 41 to 16 raises the positions scanned from 599 to 1,651.
- **`PACKAGE.json`**: bundles everything with all schema fields filled, domain "Computation" and date 2026-10-22. It contains:
  - **1 demo**: the full `demo.py`.
  - **5 algorithms**, each with pseudocode and code: building the factor base, the sign-flip certificate, first-dependency detection over \(\mathbb{F}_2\), generating discriminating test matrices with a mutation check, and large-prime pairing.
  - **4 visualization scripts**: paid work against \(q\), the \(\Psi\) bounds, a \(k_{\rm success}\) histogram, and large-prime collision growth.
  - **1 interactive HTML page** with five tabs. One tab runs a real sieve in the browser; another is a live calculator for the even-determinant rank test.
  - **A guided tour** that embeds these elements, with proofs and background in collapsible sections and links to outside references.
  - **The Phase A future directions**, lightly edited. The given text was cut off, so I closed it with a short section on the multi-polynomial and adaptive-\(B\) channels.
  - **All six Lean files from Phase A** (the five round-42 files plus the salvaged Babel-code file), with their paths.

**How it was checked**
- `demo.py`, all five algorithm snippets and the widget's code run without errors. The widget was syntax-checked and its main functions tested outside a browser. The visualization scripts run correctly with plotting switched off, because matplotlib isn't installed here.
- For the LaTeX file I checked that braces and environments balance, but it has not been through `pdflatex`.
- I did not rebuild the Lean files: a supporting library they import isn't in this project.

The files are committed and published.
